Two specialists who can each consult the other. No cycle is written down;
the cycle is what 'let them collaborate' means once it is code.

Each unbounded arrangement is run 2 times, because one run of this
measures the day rather than the design.

as intended, no limit of any kind
  depth  : [4, 4]
  tokens : ['15,687', '17,371']

one wrong subscript, no limit of any kind
  depth  : [4, 10]
  tokens : ['10,098', '25,716']

as intended, with a depth limit of 2
  depth  : 2
  tokens : 6,264
  chain  :
      supervisor -> analyst
        analyst -> researcher
      supervisor -> researcher

Across 4 runs of two arrangements that draw as the same picture, the
deepest chain was 10 agents and the most expensive answer cost 25,716 tokens —
against 10,098 for the cheapest. Same question, every time.

The miswiring is one subscript: brief the colleague with the caller's role
instead of its own, and each worker ends up consulting a copy of itself.

Today that arrangement — one wrong subscript — went deepest. Do not read much into
which one it was: the same arrangement varied by 6 levels of depth across its
own 2 runs, on the same question, with nothing changed between them.

That is the lesson, and it is not really about recursion. Neither unbounded
arrangement decided to stop; each one happened to. A run that finishes cheaply
is not evidence about the next one, so there is no version of 'we tested it
and it was fine' that covers this.

Only the last arrangement is bounded, and bounded is the only kind you can run
without watching it.
